response:
 To implement the bubble sort algorithm in C++ with minimal memory usage and potential errors, follow these steps:
1. Define a function `bubbleSort(int arr[], int n)` that takes an integer array and its size as arguments.
2. Inside the function, use two nested for loops to iterate through the array and perform the bubble sort algorithm.
3. Use constant variables or local variables instead of dynamically allocating memory to minimize memory usage.
Here's the code:
```c++
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                swap(arr[j], arr[j+1]);
            }
        }
    }
}
int main() {
    int arrSize = 6;
    int arr[arrSize] = {5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6};
    bubbleSort(arr, arrSize);
    // Print sorted array
