response:
 Based on your instruction to create a new array with considering the given conditions, here's how we can do it:

First, let's separate the items based on their `randomize` property into two groups - one for fixed position items and another for random position items.

```javascript
const fixedItems = [];
const randomItems = [];

for (const item of arr) {
  if (!item.randomize) {
    fixedItems.push(item);
  } else {
    randomItems.push(item);
  }
}
```

Now, we will create a new empty array to store the final result.

```javascript
const result = [];
```

Next, we will add the fixed position items to the result array at their respective indices.

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < fixedItems.length; i++) {
  result[fixedItems[i].position - 1] = fixedItems[i];
}
```

Finally, we will randomly place the random position items into the remaining slots in the result array. We will use Fisher-Yates shuffle algorithm to randomize the positions of random items.

```javascript
function fisherYatesShuffle(arr) {
  for (let i = arr.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
    [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
  }
}

fisherYatesShuffle(randomItems);

for (let i = 0; i < randomItems.length; i++) {
  result[fixedItems.length + i] = randomItems[i];
}
```

The final `result` array will contain all items from the original array with respect to their `randomize` property.

 To retrieve the username from a JWT (JSON Web Token) in an ASP.NET C# WebApi project, you can follow these steps:

1. First, make sure that your WebApi project uses JsonWebTokenHandler to parse and validate the JWT token. If not, install the `Jose-JWT` NuGet package using the following command in the Package Manager Console:

```sh
Install-Package Jose.Jwt
```

2. After installing the required NuGet package, create a method to extract the username from the JWT token. Here's an example of how to implement this method:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Claims;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;

public static string GetUsernameFromJwt(HttpContext context)
{
    if (context == null || string.IsNullOrEmpty(context.Request.Headers["Authorization"].ToString()))
        throw new ArgumentNullException();

    var jwtToken = context.Request.Headers["Authorization"].ToString().Split(" ")[1];

    // Assuming that your JWT token is issued by localhost and the audience is set as 'yourappname'
    var tokenValidationParameters = new TokenValidationParameters
    {
        ValidateIssuerSigningKey = true,
        ValidIssuer = "localhost",
        ValidAudience = "yourappname"
    };

    _ = context.RequestServices.TryGetService(out IJsonWebTokenDecoder decoder);
    var handler = decoder as IJsonWebTokenHandler;
    SecurityToken validatedToken;

    try
    {
        validatedToken = handler.ReadToken(jwtToken) as JwtSecurityToken;
    }
    catch (Exception)
    {
        throw new UnauthorizedAccessException("Invalid or expired token.");
    }

    return validatedToken.Principal.FindFirstValue(ClaimTypes.Name);
}
```

3. Now you have a method `GetUsernameFromJwt()` that retrieves the username from the JWT token. Use it whenever you need to access the username within your WebApi controllers or other components.

