 I'd be happy to help you write a PowerShell script for recursively checking folders to see if their Access Control Lists (ACLs) contain Security Identifiers (SIDs) that occur only in the SID history of any user or group in the Active Directory domain. Here's an outline of how you can accomplish this using PowerShell:

1. Import the required modules for Active Directory and ADObjectAccess:

```powershell
Import-Module ActiveDirectory
Import-Module ADSecurity
```

2. Define a function to get the SID History of a user or group:

```powershell
function Get-SidHistory {
    param([string]$Identity)
    $searcher = New-Object -TypeName System.DirectoryServices.DirectorySearcher
    $searcher.FilterLDAPFilter = "(samAccountName=$Identity)"
    $userOrGroup = $searcher.FindOne()
    $sidHistory = @()
    if ($userOrGroup) {
        $object = $userOrGroup.Properties["sIDHistory"].Value
        foreach ($item in $object) {
            $sidHistory += New-Object System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier($item, $false)
        }
    }
    return $sidHistory
}
```

3. Define a function to check if an SID is present in the given ACL:

```powershell
function Check-AclForSids {
    param(
        [string]$Path,
        [hashtable]$SidHistory
    )
    $acl = Get-Acl $Path
    $sidsInAcl = @()
    foreach ($access in $acl.Access) {
        $identityReference = $access.IdentityReference
        if ($identityReference.Translate([System.Security.Principal.NTAccount]).Value -eq "") {
            $sid = $identityReference.Translate([System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier]).Value
            $sidsInAcl += $sid
        }
    }
    foreach ($sid in $sidsInAcl) {
        if ($SidHistory.ContainsKey($sid)) {
            Write-Host "SID $sid found in ACL of $Path"
